# Memory Preservation And Reuse

## Purpose
- Preserve reusable project knowledge across sessions and agents.
- Make prior lessons discoverable before doing new implementation work.

## Memory-First Execution Rule
- Before starting execution, code generation, refactoring, or debugging, check `.cursor/memory/MEMORY.md`.
- Use `MEMORY.md` as the playbook/index to decide which topic and lesson files to read.
- Apply relevant prior lessons while planning and implementing.

## Memory Structure
- `.cursor/memory/MEMORY.md` is the top-level playbook and routing guide.
- `.cursor/memory/<topic>/` folders group related knowledge domains.
- `.cursor/memory/<topic>/*.md` stores individual lessons, decisions, pitfalls, and reusable patterns.

## What To Store
- Add lessons from reusable experiences during implementation, refactor, debugging, and execution.
- Always store items the user explicitly asks to preserve.
- Capture concise context: problem, solution, why it worked, and when to reuse it.

## Update Discipline
- If you discover a new reusable lesson, update the right topic file (or create one).
- If a new topic appears, add a topic folder and update `MEMORY.md` so others can find it.
- Keep entries short, actionable, and searchable.
